# Linux-only native WebView bindings, required to build the frozen Linux app.
#
# pywebview's GTK backend imports `gi` at module scope, so PyInstaller can only
# bundle a real WebView when PyGObject is importable in the build environment.
# PyGObject publishes no wheels -- pip compiles it, which needs libgirepository
# and libcairo headers plus pkg-config. Those belong to the packaging job only,
# so this lock is kept out of requirements-dev-lock.txt: installing it there
# would make every pull-request checkout fail before it runs a single test.
#
# The ceiling is load-bearing: PyGObject 3.52+ builds against
# libgirepository-2.0-dev, which ubuntu-22.04 does not provide.
#
# The constraint is not optional: without it uv re-resolves the whole graph and
# this file silently ships different versions to the Linux bundle than the
# macOS and Windows bundles built from requirements-dev-lock.txt. Regenerate
# both locks together, then diff the version lines to confirm they still agree.
#
# Regenerate with:
#
#   uv pip compile --universal --generate-hashes --python-version 3.12 \
#       --output-file requirements-linux-gui-lock.txt requirements-linux-gui.in
-c requirements-dev-lock.txt
-r requirements-dev.in
PyGObject>=3.48,<3.52; sys_platform == 'linux'
